# Command portman

## What does this command do:

A comprehensive port management tool for developers with CLI and GUI interfaces

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: npm install -g portkeeper
For Raspberry Pi install using: npm install -g portkeeper
For Mac install using: npm install -g portkeeper
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: npm install -g portkeeper

## Command path

For Fedora run: /home/kinscoe/.local/bin/portman
For Raspberry Pi run: ~/.local/bin/portman
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/portman
For Mac container run: /home/kinscoe/.local/bin/portman

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://github.com/ahmadzein/portkeeper

## Command options

# Check if a port is reserved or in use
portman check <port> [--json]

# Reserve a port for a project
portman reserve <port> [-n <name>] [-d <description>] [-t <tags...>] [--auto-release] [--json]

# List all reserved and in-use ports
portman list [-s reserved|in-use|free] [-p <project>] [-t <tags...>] [--json]

# Release one or more reserved ports
portman release <ports...> [--json]

# Kill process using specified port(s)
portman kill <ports...> [-f] [--json]

# Scan for all ports currently in use
portman scan [--range <start-end>] [--json]

# Request and reserve multiple available ports automatically
portman request <count> [-n <name>] [-d <desc>] [-t <tags>] [--start <port>] [--end <port>] [-r|--random] [--json]

# Export port configuration to JSON
portman export [-o <file>] [--pretty]

# Import port configuration from JSON
portman import [file] [--merge] [--dry-run] [--json]

# Launch the GUI application (requires: npm install -g portkeeper --include=optional)
portman gui
